        <fig id="article-20859.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>T-Wave Morphology. A comparison of different T-wave morphologies on an electrocardiogram: Normal, Flat, Inverted, Tall, and Biphasic. These variations from the normal, upright, rounded T wave are clinically significant, often suggesting underlying conditions such as ischemia or electrolyte imbalance. Contributed by&#x000a0;S Bhimji, MD</p>
          </caption>
